    "Stem": "ASC 740 requires recognition of deferred taxes for:",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "ASC 740 requires deferred tax recognition for all temporary differences between book and tax bases of assets and liabilities. Temporary differences reverse in future periods (e.g., depreciation method differences, warranty accruals, bad debt reserves). Permanent differences (e.g., municipal bond interest, nondeductible fines, dividends-received deduction) never reverse and therefore never create deferred tax assets or liabilities. The distinction between temporary and permanent differences is one of the most frequently tested income tax concepts on the CMA exam.",
    "QuestionID": "P1E-A-055",
    "question_state": "Certified",
    "Part1OnlyFlag": true,
    "ReviewNote": "If missed or marked, review the linked study materials and rework the underlying concept without looking at the answer.",
    "ItemStyle": "single-select",
    "ExplanationWrongA": "ASC 740 does not recognize deferred taxes for permanent differences because they never reverse in future periods. Permanent differences arise when an item is recognized in book income but never in taxable income (or vice versa), such as municipal bond interest, nondeductible fines, or the dividends-received deduction.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "Permanent differences are excluded from deferred tax accounting under ASC 740; deferred taxes are recognized only for temporary differences that will reverse in future periods. Including all differences (as B suggests) would incorrectly create deferred tax entries for items that never reverse.",
