    "Stem": "Asset turnover ratio =:",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "The asset turnover ratio measures how efficiently a company uses its assets to generate sales revenue and is a core component of the DuPont analysis framework under CMA Part 1 Section C.2 (Performance Management). The formula is Asset Turnover = Sales divided by Average Total Assets. A higher ratio indicates more efficient asset utilization — the company generates more sales per dollar invested in assets. Capital-intensive industries (manufacturing, utilities) typically have lower asset turnover ratios than service-based or technology companies with lighter asset bases. In the DuPont decomposition, Return on Investment (ROI) = Profit Margin times Asset Turnover, meaning overall profitability depends on both margin efficiency and asset efficiency. A common exam trap is inverting the ratio as Assets / Sales, which produces a different result and loses the efficiency interpretation. This ratio is tested alongside profit margin and ROI to assess whether management is deploying company resources effectively.",
