    "Stem": "Prime costs are:",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "Prime costs consist of direct materials and direct labor — the two primary inputs that are directly traceable to the finished product. Prime costs are distinguished from conversion costs, which consist of direct labor plus manufacturing overhead (the costs to convert raw materials into finished goods). Direct labor is the overlap between the two classifications: it is included in both prime costs and conversion costs. The relationship is: Total Manufacturing Cost = Direct Materials + Direct Labor + MOH = Prime Costs + Conversion Costs - Direct Labor. These cost classifications are foundational to manufacturing cost accounting and are tested under CMA Part 1 Section D.1 (Cost Management — cost measurement concepts). A common exam trap is confusing prime costs with conversion costs or with total manufacturing costs. The mnemonic 'Prime = Primary inputs (DM + DL)' helps distinguish prime costs from conversion costs.",
