    "ExplanationCorrect": "COSO identifies several inherent limitations of internal control: human error, collusion, management override, and cost-benefit constraints. Management override is classified as an inherent limitation — not a control deficiency — because even in a well-designed system of internal control, management has the authority to override prescribed policies or procedures for what it believes to be legitimate business purposes. No internal control system can completely prevent management from exercising this override authority, because management by definition sits above the control structure. A control deficiency exists when the design or operation of a control does not allow management or employees, in the normal course of performing their assigned functions, to prevent or detect misstatements on a timely basis. The distinction matters for exam purposes: management override is an inherent limitation of any internal control system, while a missing segregation of duties, for example, is a control deficiency that can and should be remedied. The best mitigation for management override risk is a strong control environment — particularly an active, independent board of directors and audit committee that provides oversight of management's activities.",
