    "ExplanationWrongA": "This classification is incorrect because none of the three controls is primarily detective. Detective controls identify errors or irregularities after they occur (e.g., reconciliation, review of exception reports). Dual signatures prevent unauthorized orders before they are placed, three-way matching prevents improper payments before they are disbursed, and account lockout prevents unauthorized access before it succeeds. A candidate selecting this option may be confusing the verification aspect of three-way matching (which compares documents) with detective control timing, but verification performed before payment is preventive in nature.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "Dual signatures are preventive, not detective — they stop unauthorized orders before they occur, not after. Account lockout is preventive, not corrective — it prevents access before a breach occurs rather than fixing the consequences of a breach. Three-way matching is correctly classified as preventive. A candidate selecting this option may be incorrectly applying a 'approval = detective' mental model. The distinction depends on timing: if the control blocks the event before it occurs, it is preventive; if it flags it after, it is detective.",
    "ExplanationWrongD": "Account lockout is preventive, not detective, because it prevents the third (and subsequent) login attempts from succeeding. A detective control would log the failed attempts but allow them to continue, or alert an administrator after the fact. The lockout mechanism proactively stops the undesirable event (unauthorized access), making it preventive by definition. The candidate may be incorrectly associating lockout with detection because it requires failed-attempt detection logic to trigger, but the control action itself (locking the account) occurs before access is granted — the definitional test for preventive classification.",
