    "Stem": "Under the COSO Internal Control — Integrated Framework, the risk assessment component involves which of the following?",
    "CorrectChoice": "D",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "Risk assessment is the second component of the COSO Internal Control framework and involves a dynamic and iterative process for identifying and analyzing risks to the achievement of the entity's objectives. This process forms the basis for determining how risks should be managed. COSO emphasizes that risk assessment includes: (1) establishing clear objectives at the entity and sub-entity levels to enable risk identification; (2) identifying risks to the achievement of those objectives across the entity; (3) analyzing risks to estimate their significance and likelihood; (4) assessing fraud risk, which includes considering incentives and pressures, opportunities, and attitudes and rationalizations; and (5) identifying and assessing changes in the external environment, business model, and leadership that could significantly affect the system of internal control. Risk assessment is not a one-time event but an ongoing process that responds to changes in operating conditions, regulatory requirements, technology, and organizational structure. The assessment of risk is foundational — it determines which control activities are necessary, where monitoring should focus, and what information needs to flow through the communication channels. Without effective risk assessment, the entity cannot target its control resources efficiently.",
