    "Stem": "Internal control, no matter how well designed and operated, can provide only reasonable assurance — not absolute assurance — regarding achievement of an entity's objectives. Which of the following is the primary reason for this inherent limitation?",
    "CorrectChoice": "A",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "Internal control can only provide reasonable assurance because of several inherent limitations that cannot be eliminated regardless of control design quality. The most fundamental limitations are: (1) human judgment can be faulty, and controls can break down due to simple error or mistake; (2) controls can be circumvented by collusion — two or more individuals acting together to override segregation of duties; and (3) management has the ability to override internal controls. Additionally, the cost of a control should not exceed its expected benefit, creating a cost-benefit constraint on control implementation. These are not defects in control design — they are intrinsic to any system that involves human decision-making. The 'reasonable assurance' concept appears throughout the CMA exam framework and is important because it sets realistic expectations: internal controls reduce but do not eliminate the risk of not achieving objectives.",
