    "Stem": "Westlake Supply Company is restructuring its accounts payable department. The department manager proposes assigning each clerk full responsibility for a specific vendor set — including receiving vendor invoices, entering invoices into the system, preparing checks for those invoices, and reconciling the related vendor statements. Which internal control principle does this assignment violate?",
    "CorrectChoice": "B",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "This assignment violates segregation of duties, which is a foundational preventive control. The principle requires that incompatible functions — specifically authorization, custody, recording, and reconciliation — be divided among different individuals to reduce the risk of error or fraud. In this scenario, a single clerk would: (1) record the transaction (enter invoice), (2) have custody of assets (prepare checks), and (3) perform reconciliation (reconcile vendor statements). This combination creates an opportunity for an employee to record fictitious invoices, prepare corresponding payments, and conceal the fraud through the reconciliation process. The classic incompatible pairing is recording and custody — if one person both records a transaction and controls the related asset, misappropriation risk substantially increases. The CMA exam frequently tests the ability to identify which specific duties are incompatible in a given scenario and why the combination creates risk.",
