    "Stem": "During the preparation of its quarterly Form 10-Q filing, the CEO and CFO of Delta Manufacturing must certify several items. One certification required by Section 302 of the Sarbanes-Oxley Act that is NOT explicitly required by the separate Section 906 criminal certification is:",
    "CorrectChoice": "C",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "SOX Section 302 (codified at 15 U.S.C. § 7241) requires the CEO and CFO to certify, among other things, that they are responsible for establishing and maintaining disclosure controls and procedures and internal control over financial reporting, and that they have evaluated the effectiveness of those disclosure controls and procedures within 90 days before the report. Section 906 (18 U.S.C. § 1350) is a separate criminal certification that the periodic report fully complies with SEC requirements and fairly presents the financial condition and results of operations — it carries criminal penalties (up to $5 million fine and/or 20 years imprisonment for knowing violations). The key distinction tested: Section 302 requires certification about the officers' responsibility for and evaluation of internal controls; Section 906 focuses on the accuracy and fairness of the report itself. Both sections' certifications must accompany periodic filings (Forms 10-K and 10-Q), but the content and legal consequences differ. The CMA exam tests SOX provisions because they fundamentally changed management accountability for internal control.",
