    "Stem": "Under Generally Accepted Auditing Standards (GAAS), an auditor issues an opinion indicating that the financial statements contain material misstatements that are both material and pervasive to the financial statements as a whole. This type of audit opinion is a(n):",
    "CorrectChoice": "D",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "Under AU-C Section 705, there are three types of modified audit opinions: (1) qualified opinion — the financial statements are fairly presented except for a specific matter that is material but not pervasive; (2) adverse opinion — the financial statements contain misstatements that are both material and pervasive, meaning the statements as a whole are not fairly presented; and (3) disclaimer of opinion — the auditor cannot obtain sufficient appropriate audit evidence and the possible effects are both material and pervasive. An adverse opinion is the most severe type of opinion because it communicates that the financial statements should not be relied upon — they do not present fairly the financial position, results of operations, or cash flows in conformity with the applicable financial reporting framework. The distinction between 'material' and 'material and pervasive' is critical: materiality relates to magnitude (could a user's decision be affected?), while pervasiveness relates to scope (does the issue affect the financial statements as a whole or is it confined to specific accounts or disclosures?). The CMA exam tests knowledge of audit opinion types because management accountants interact with external auditors and must understand what each opinion type communicates to stakeholders.",
