    "Stem": "Coastal State Bank is evaluating its internal control design for the commercial loan approval process. The chief risk officer notes that loan applications below $250,000 are reviewed by a single credit analyst using a checklist, while applications above $250,000 require committee review. The CRO is considering replacing the checklist review with an automated scoring model that applies consistent decision rules to all applications regardless of dollar amount. When comparing manual controls (current checklist review) to automated controls (proposed scoring model), which of the following is an inherent limitation of manual controls that automated controls specifically address?",
    "CorrectChoice": "B",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "The fundamental trade-off between manual and automated controls addresses several dimensions. Manual controls rely on human judgment and diligence, which introduces specific limitations: (1) inconsistency — different individuals apply the same checklist differently, or the same individual applies it inconsistently across time due to fatigue, distraction, or interpretation differences; (2) skill dependency — effectiveness depends on the individual's training, experience, and attention to detail on a given day; (3) scalability constraints — manual review processes become bottlenecked as transaction volumes increase. Automated controls address inconsistency directly by applying identical programmed logic to every transaction without variation. Under COSO Principle 12 (Control Activities) and Principle 16 (Monitoring), an effective control system typically combines both types, leveraging automated controls for consistency and volume handling while relying on manual controls for judgment-intensive decisions and anomaly detection. The CMA exam expects candidates to understand the strengths and limitations of each type rather than viewing automation as universally superior — automated controls introduce their own risks, including: programming errors that propagate across all transactions, susceptibility to unauthorized code changes, and inability to exercise professional judgment in unusual situations.",
